# **The movie that loses**, kept because losing is the finding.
#
# BLHR is level 45 as it starts. This route is aimed at the type-$3A object at
# (230,1044) -- collision id $30, whose jump-table entry is `$80:FAA4` -- and it
# reaches it: at frame 3790 the player is at (222,1050), eight pixels away on X
# and six on Y, which `$80:BEF1` counts as touching.
#
# **He does not get it, and `zamn_headless --records` says why in one frame:**
#
#     $1AB6  $8001    222  1050   $05   $00     <- the player
#     $1A7A  $8011    230  1050   $04   $24     <- a monster, on the same object
#     $1AA2  $8001    230  1044   $30   $26     <- the object
#
# `$80:CAEE` accepts three collision ids and the third, $0004, is the monster
# side. `actor_overlap_pass` walks pairs from the end of the display list, the
# monster sorted later than the player, and the object's `ACTOR_COLLIDE_ID` is
# cleared on that first ask -- so the player's handler is called with an id of
# zero, which is `$80:F87A`, a bare RTS.
#
# So this movie is the only input in the project that takes **`object_spent`**
# and **`player_no_effect`**, and it takes each of them three times. Both are
# paths that only exist because two things can want the same object on the same
# frame, and neither had ever run.
#
# It is also fifty-six legs where the same target refitted is fourteen, because it
# was cut before `tools/fit_route.py` learned the game's eight-pixel box: it
# reached that box on leg 36 and spent twenty more trying to stand on the exact
# pixel. The tail after frame 3803 is that thrashing, cut off here.
#
# `zamn_cosim verify -f 3810` checks 69,768 calls with no divergence.

